# Cloudflare Workers secrets reference
#
# Unlike a typical .env file, these are NOT read from disk by the deployed
# Worker -- Cloudflare Workers secrets must be set via the Wrangler CLI (or
# the dashboard), which encrypts them and makes them available as `env.*`
# bindings at runtime.
#
# FOR LOCAL DEVELOPMENT (`npm run dev`), wrangler reads secrets from a
# `.dev.vars` file in this directory instead (gitignored; never commit it).
# Create one with the same variable names as below, e.g.:
#
#   JWT_ACCESS_SECRET=any-random-string-for-local-dev-only
#   JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=a-different-random-string
#   TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=1x0000000000000000000000000000000AA
#
# (That Turnstile key is Cloudflare's official "always passes" test secret
# for local/CI use -- see developers.cloudflare.com/turnstile/troubleshooting/testing.)
# Without a `.dev.vars` file, JWT_ACCESS_SECRET will be empty and every
# login attempt will fail with a 500 error from the JWT signing step.
#
# FOR PRODUCTION, set each secret with:
#   npx wrangler secret put <NAME>
# You'll be prompted to paste the value interactively.

# JWT_ACCESS_SECRET
#   Signs short-lived (15 min) access tokens. Generate with:
#     openssl rand -base64 48
JWT_ACCESS_SECRET=

# JWT_REFRESH_SECRET
#   Currently reserved for future use if refresh tokens move from opaque
#   random strings to signed JWTs. Generate the same way as above; keep
#   it DIFFERENT from JWT_ACCESS_SECRET so a leak of one doesn't
#   compromise the other token type.
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=

# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY
#   From your Cloudflare Turnstile widget (dash.cloudflare.com > Turnstile).
#   Used server-side to verify CAPTCHA tokens after repeated failed logins.
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=

# ADMIN_SETUP_TOKEN
#   Reserved for a future one-time hosted setup flow. The current
#   recommended way to create your first admin user is:
#     npm run create-admin -- <username> <email> <password>
#   which hashes the password locally and writes directly to D1.
ADMIN_SETUP_TOKEN=
